00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:08.750
when it first started the primary data set that&nbsp;
was available was the young adult human connectome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:16.190
project and in that data set which at that point&nbsp;
we were playing with had about 500 subjects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:23.310
so that was already an a few orders of magnitude&nbsp;
larger than most data sets in people's labs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:30.190
and this raised various kinds of challenges for us&nbsp;
one of the things we were interested in was to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:37.390
and understand whether resting state functional&nbsp;
data could be used to do better alignment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:43.070
individuals to each other remember very early on&nbsp;
i said one of the things not people normally did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:49.550
was to take volumetric images and align&nbsp;
one brain to another or to a template&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:57.230
we found that to be difficult at least in&nbsp;
those early days and used region based analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.230 --> 00:20:02.590
to do things but anatomical regions while they&nbsp;
govern some aspects of functionality are not an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:09.230
identical map for all aspects of functionality&nbsp;
at the macro scale level so resting state data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:14.910
which literally involves somebody lying in a&nbsp;
scanner and you're monitoring their brain activity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:23.070
as it spontaneously fluctuates over time provides&nbsp;
some amount of structure to the patterns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:30.270
emerge and this gives rise to specific types of&nbsp;
networks and our question was could we use this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:36.270
look at how two people are similar to each other&nbsp;
and we relied on some mathematical techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:43.790
which creates this matrix of correspondences&nbsp;
now the human connectome project data allows us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:50.350
to do this for every single individual here's an&nbsp;
example of looking at it for three individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:55.310
they may have different regions in the brain that&nbsp;
relate similarly to each other but you can kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.310 --> 00:21:02.510
of align them all together using a procrustes or&nbsp;
other types of transforms into a common space now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:09.070
the challenge here was that the human connectom&nbsp;
project data released data that were about 90 000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:18.030
rows or points and if you construct this matrix&nbsp;
that would be a 90 000 square matrix if you stored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:24.830
the 90 000 square matrix on a computer it will as&nbsp;
a floating point number or a matrix of floating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:33.070
point numbers that will take about 64 gigabytes of&nbsp;
ram at that point in time not every computer had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:42.110
64 gigabytes of ram and if you couple this now&nbsp;
with 500 subjects worth of data you're getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:47.870
into a place where your standard computational&nbsp;
tools are no longer feasible to do some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:54.270
things so we came up with various ways and&nbsp;
efficiencies to deal with that scale of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.270 --> 00:22:01.630
and that changed the mindset of what computational&nbsp;
infrastructure we should be investing in even at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:10.270
mit we were able to at that time think about ways&nbsp;
in which the future of computation were to evolve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:17.230
and preempt the construction of our compute&nbsp;
cluster to leverage larger memory sizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:21.870
even though most people weren't really&nbsp;
solving problems that needed that much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:28.510
and this was a big boost our benefit at that time&nbsp;
we were able to run this human connectome project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:35.870
analysis where we aligned people to a common space&nbsp;
using functional architectures and this itself led&nbsp;&nbsp;

